{
  "source_head": "109aab5063dc056f794ef2c98f8ba2d6ca0717a9",
  "scope": "Partial semantic review of 116 guides and historical acceptance records at source109aab5; not exhaustive source validation, external-reference review or independent deployment",
  "all_documents_reviewed": false,
  "actual_public_renewal_accepted": false,
  "findings": [
    "Admission, durable CSR, role, CA account, private snapshots, HTTP-01 and finalization responsibilities remain separate.",
    "CA protocol fixtures, logically aged renewal, real native application and real public renewal-clock proof remain distinct.",
    "Explicit consent, original candidate/account identity, no raw secret logging, retained ambiguous outcomes and fail-closed boundary statements remain stated.",
    "Relay authority preparation/custody assertions, publication barriers, retained-operation recovery, served-TLS verification and offline quarantine remain separate; no automatic offline bypass is documented.",
    "Protected issuer host documentation separately states privileged host trust, narrow SQL identities, cleanup gates, original job/order/CSR retention and the actual fixture limitations. The public policy example still has the documented nine fields.",
    "Quickstart omitted the explicit --network-subnet argument despite asking the operator to select a subnet. Both init examples now pass the selected value; doctor examples are unchanged. This correction is a documentation follow-up, not a mutation of the frozen candidate.",
    "Certificate automation overview ended with a stale blanket protected-host-restoration gap. The follow-up now links merged isolated recovery while retaining public operator, real renewal-clock and durable encrypted off-host custody requirements. Original staged preparation/recovery failures remain historical failures.",
    "English/Chinese entry points, SAML dependency/admission/provider guides and current console/deployment API boundaries retain source-specific acceptance, no email-only binding, immutable grants/revocation and public/mobile/release gaps.",
    "The full deadline/recovery history distinguishes SQL and console/public clocks, scheduled reboot delay, real recovery, irreversible operations and scoped later success without relabeling failed trials.",
    "Image guidance now identifies old Alpine3.23 advisory/index links as historical context, distinct from the documented current Caddy3.24 base; actual Dockerfile/module pins and required scans are unchanged.",
    "Authentication defaults and bounded verification/limiter policy agree with the inspected current constants. Backup scheduling, receipt/hash versus actual recovery, host lease ownership and bootstrap non-replay boundaries remain distinct. HTTP-01 publication guidance now links the implemented host worker rather than saying its startup is future work; public custody and renewal-clock acceptance remain open.",
    "Agent/capability/plugin guides retain remote.web-only authority, separate os-frp state, current guard/lock ordering and unsigned-versus-formal package boundaries. The control-plane command table incorrectly named the admin-bootstrap Compose service as a CLI command; it now names the implemented admin create command and its service. Historical browser and FreeBSD contract scope remain separate from installed/public acceptance.",
    "Historical enrollment and certificate records retain their own sources, intentional image reuse boundaries, private fixture roots and the owner-approved old 1GiB allocation; those exceptions do not apply to this host or establish production sizing. Failed offline application, model-normalization diagnosis, later private-CA renewal/rollback and public-CA initial issuance are distinct outcomes. Actual public renewal-clock acceptance remains open. Separate Dex/Keycloak fixtures explicitly label SQL inventory rows and do not claim public providers or native tunnels.",
    "The public console trial explicitly excludes clean-host and mobile-carrier acceptance. Relay transport, native distribution, admitted signer transitions and retirement are distinct staged records; initial timeout and later daemon-deadline reproductions are not relabeled. The old explicit lab SQL administration and resource exceptions are historical evidence, not authorization to repeat those operations in a new environment. Public chain normalization retains parser/identity/trust refusals and read-only original-order diagnostics.",
    "Thirteen original plugin progress records describe staged development package versions. Source-only key/CSR/journal controls, Core application and daemon/boot recovery, enrollment/lifecycle, local breaker and WAN/coexistence observations remain distinct. Their earlier future-work statements and old 10/30 heartbeat/compiler records are historical checkpoint scope, not instructions to downgrade the current 1/3 profile, compiler or authority boundaries. Key export, native backup history and cleanup limits remain explicit.",
    "W3/W4 milestone records state their accepted old host/public-forwarding scope and preserve earlier failed observations. Those owner-approved historical environments do not authorize this shared host to expose ports. Restricted dump restoration, original six-service/client recovery and shared-token incident checks retain distinct sources and archive/upload limitations; later protected-host restoration does not retroactively broaden them. Normal Open with a measured source, unchanged grants and independently verified routes remains required."
  ],
